---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// New page. NTv3 is a genuinely separate paper from the Nature Methods Nucleotide Transformer;
// the /papers/nucleotide-transformer/ page previously carried an NT3 row with numbers that its
// own source paper does not contain, because the NT3 "download" on disk was a Cloudflare block
// page. Written against the InstaDeep PDF (107 pp).
// Checked by `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug nucleotide-transformer-v3`.
const title = "A Foundational Model for Joint Sequence-Function Multi-Species Modeling at Scale for Long-Range Genomic Prediction";
const description = "Technical summary of Nucleotide Transformer v3 (Boshar, Evans, Tang et al., InstaDeep, 2025): a U-Net backbone with single-base tokenization and 1 Mb context, pretrained on 9 trillion base pairs and post-trained on ~16,000 functional tracks across 24 species.";
const readingTime = "26 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">DNA Foundation Models & Long-Range Genomics</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Preprint · 23 December 2025 (InstaDeep)</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">Nucleotide Transformer v3: Joint Sequence-Function Modeling for Long-Range Genomic Prediction</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Sam Boshar*, Benjamin Evans*, Ziqi Tang*, Armand Picard, Yanis Adel, Franziska K. Lorbeer, Chandana Rajesh, Tristan Karch, Shawn Sidbon, David Emms … (InstaDeep)</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> InstaDeep</div>
        <div><strong>Full title:</strong> <em>{title}</em></div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      The DNA foundation-model field had split into models that read long context, models that predict
      functional tracks, and models that generate sequence — each with its own architecture, tokenization and
      training regime. <strong>NTv3</strong> unifies <strong>representation learning, functional-track and
      genome-annotation prediction, and controllable sequence generation within a common backbone</strong>. A
      <strong>U-Net-like architecture</strong> makes <strong>single-base tokenization</strong> affordable at
      contexts up to <strong>1 Mb</strong>; pretraining runs on <strong>9 trillion base pairs from
      OpenGenome2</strong>; and post-training adds supervision from <strong>~16,000 functional tracks and
      annotation labels across 24 animal and plant species</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. The Fragmentation NTv3 Is Answering</a></li>
        <li><a href="#architecture">2. The U-Net Backbone and Single-Base Tokenization</a></li>
        <li><a href="#pretraining">3. Pretraining: 9 Trillion Base Pairs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#posttraining">4. Post-Training: The Joint Objective</a></li>
        <li><a href="#results">5. Results Against Borzoi</a></li>
        <li><a href="#context">6. Does 1 Mb Actually Help?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">7. Mathematical Formulation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations & Relationship to NT v1/v2</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. The Fragmentation NTv3 Is Answering</h2>
      <p>
        The paper's framing is that existing models differ in <strong>architectures and training
        regimes</strong> in ways that limit <strong>the ability to combine long-context, base-resolution
        prediction, functional modeling, and controllable generation within a single efficient framework that
        generalizes across organisms and modalities</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each capability had been bought by giving up another. Sequence-to-function models like Borzoi read
        long context but are supervised on tracks and do not generate. DNA language models generate and
        represent but historically read short windows. NTv3's claim is that one backbone can carry all of it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. The U-Net Backbone and Single-Base Tokenization</h2>

      <h3>Why single-base tokenization is the hard requirement</h3>
      <p>
        Its predecessor used six-mer tokens, which bound resolution: a model consuming six nucleotides per
        token cannot cleanly express an event at a single base. NTv3 uses
        <strong>single-base tokenization</strong>, so every position is individually addressable.
      </p>

      <h3>Why that forces a U-Net</h3>
      <p>
        Single-base tokens at 1 Mb means a sequence of a million tokens, which plain attention cannot afford.
        The <strong>U-Net-like architecture</strong> is what makes it tractable: downsample to a coarse
        representation where long-range interactions are cheap to model, then upsample back to base
        resolution, with skip connections carrying local detail past the bottleneck.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> The trade the architecture resolves
        </div>
        <p>
          Resolution and range pull against each other under quadratic attention: you can have base
          resolution over a short window, or coarse resolution over a long one. The U-Net's contribution is
          that the two live at different depths of the same network — long-range reasoning in the compressed
          middle, base-resolution output at the edges — rather than being traded against each other in a
          single flat sequence.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="pretraining" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. Pretraining: 9 Trillion Base Pairs</h2>
      <p>
        NTv3 is pretrained on <strong>9 trillion base pairs from OpenGenome2</strong> using
        <strong>base-resolution masked language modeling</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        This corpus belongs to NTv3 and to no other model in the family — a point worth stating because the
        earlier Nucleotide Transformer was pretrained on 3,202 human genomes and 850 multispecies genomes,
        which is a different and much smaller corpus. Attributing 9 trillion base pairs to the NT suite as a
        whole conflates two papers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="posttraining" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Post-Training: The Joint Objective</h2>
      <p>
        After pretraining, NTv3 undergoes <strong>post-training with a joint objective that integrates
        continued self-supervision with supervised learning</strong> on <strong>~16,000 functional tracks and
        annotation labels from 24 animal and plant species</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two design choices are worth separating out. <strong>Continued self-supervision during supervised
        training</strong> is a hedge against catastrophic forgetting: a purely supervised phase would let the
        representation collapse toward whatever the tracks measure. And <strong>24 animal and plant
        species</strong> makes the supervised signal cross-kingdom, which is what supports the claim of
        generalizing across organisms rather than fitting human assay idiosyncrasies.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Stage</th>
              <th>Objective</th>
              <th>Data</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pretraining</strong></td>
              <td>Base-resolution masked language modeling</td>
              <td><strong>9 trillion bp</strong>, OpenGenome2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Post-training</strong></td>
              <td>Joint: continued self-supervision + supervised</td>
              <td><strong>~16,000 functional tracks</strong> and annotation labels, <strong>24 species</strong></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="results" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. Results Against Borzoi</h2>
      <p>
        After post-training, NTv3 <strong>achieves state-of-the-art accuracy for functional-track prediction
        and genome annotation across species</strong>, outperforming leading sequence-to-function and
        foundation-model baselines.
      </p>
      <p>
        The comparison the paper foregrounds is <strong>NTv3 650M (post) against Borzoi</strong>, described as
        <strong>the leading sequence-to-function model</strong>. The choice of a 650M-parameter checkpoint for
        the headline comparison is itself informative: the argument is about architecture, corpus and training
        regime rather than about being the largest model in the room.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="context" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. Does 1 Mb Actually Help?</h2>
      <p>
        A long context window is only worth its cost if the model uses it. The paper tests this directly and
        reports that the <strong>1 Mb setting yields gains over 30 kb in human and other selected
        species</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the necessary control. Enhancers act over hundreds of kilobases and topological domains span
        megabases, so there is a biological reason to expect a gain — but a model can be given a 1 Mb window
        and still behave as though it read 30 kb. Demonstrating improvement across the range, in more than one
        species, is what distinguishes a used context window from an advertised one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulation</h2>

      <h3>Pretraining</h3>
      <p>
        Masked language modelling at base resolution over a window of length <Katex tex="L" /> up to
        <Katex tex="10^6" />, with masked index set <Katex tex="\mathcal{M}" />:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLM}} = -\sum_{i \in \mathcal{M}} \log p_{\theta}\big(x_i \mid x_{\setminus \mathcal{M}}\big), \qquad x_i \in \{A, C, G, T\}" />
      </div>
      <p>
        Note the vocabulary against its predecessor's <Katex tex="4^6 = 4096" /> six-mers: four tokens, one
        base each.
      </p>

      <h3>The joint post-training objective</h3>
      <p>
        Self-supervision is retained alongside supervised track and annotation prediction:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}} = \lambda_{\text{ssl}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLM}} + \lambda_{\text{track}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{track}} + \lambda_{\text{ann}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{annotation}}" />
      </div>
      <p>
        The <Katex tex="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLM}}" /> term surviving into post-training is the structural
        difference from a conventional fine-tune, and it is what keeps one backbone serving representation,
        prediction and generation at once.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations & Relationship to NT v1/v2</h2>

      <h3>Status</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>This is a preprint</strong>, dated 23 December 2025, not peer reviewed. Benchmark claims should be read accordingly.</li>
        <li><strong>Cost.</strong> Nine trillion base pairs of pretraining plus ~16,000-track post-training is not a reproducible budget for most groups; the practical artefact is the released checkpoint, not the recipe.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What the supervision inherits</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Functional tracks are assays.</strong> Post-training on ~16,000 tracks imports every bias of the experiments that produced them — cell types run, conditions chosen, antibodies used. State-of-the-art track prediction means predicting those measurements well, which is not the same as predicting function.</li>
        <li><strong>24 species is broad but structured.</strong> Cross-kingdom coverage is a real advance over human-only supervision; it remains a sample of sequenced, annotated organisms.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Relationship to the earlier Nucleotide Transformer
        </div>
        <p>
          NTv3 is a <strong>different paper with a different corpus and a different architecture</strong> from
          the Nature Methods <a href="/papers/nucleotide-transformer/" style="color: var(--color-accent);">Nucleotide
          Transformer</a>, which introduced both the original models (six-mer tokens, up to 6 kb) and the
          NT-v2 models (12 kb, rotary embeddings, 50M–500M parameters). There is <strong>no separate "NT2"
          publication</strong>: the v2 models belong to that paper. Numbers should not be moved between the
          two — 9 trillion base pairs and 1 Mb context are NTv3's alone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
